45s, footage shows an adult Indian rhinoceros with its calf drinking at a muddy waterhole; at 461s, a close-up of human fingernails illustrates the keratin structure shared with hooves; and at 469s, a split-screen compares a brown horse and a rhino face-to-face. At 477s, a rhino is shown wallowing contentedly in murky water.\n\nThe segment takes a lighter turn at 493s with a brief cutaway to a classroom scene where a teacher points to a whiteboard displaying a horse head sketch, a question mark, and a whimsical cartoon unicorn surrounded by stars and moons, while students observe. The host returns to wrap up the segment, maintaining an engaging, educational tone throughout. The SciShow logo appears in the bottom corner of most frames.', 'similarity score': 0.5562642812728882}, {'clip_277_397': 'clip_277_397', 'text': 'This SciShow segment explores the genetic and developmental biology behind animal horns and antlers. \n\nThe video opens with a scientific diagram (277s) illustrating embryonic neural plate development—showing the formation of neural folds, grooves, and the neural tube/neural crest. This transitions to footage of a moose from behind (285s) with on-screen text explaining modifications to the **OTOP3 gene**, which produces proteins associated with **biomineralization**.\n\nA bald, bearded host in a grey flamingo-patterned shirt appears against a blue studio background (293s-317s), gesturing while explaining genetics. The text **"OLIG1"** appears on screen (301s) as he discusses specific genes. The video cuts to nature footage of a pale deer with striking blue eyes and impressive antlers (325s), followed by a majestic ram with large curved horns staring at the camera (333s).\n\nAnother scientific diagram appears (341s)—credited to John Inge Johnsen, Cecilia Dyberg, and Malin Wickström—showing neural crest cell migration and differentiation pathways. This is followed by a black goat with small curved horns appearing to smile at the camera (349s).\n\nThe host returns (357s-381s), using varied hand gestures to emphasize points about evolutionary biology and gene inheritance. The segment concludes (389s) with the host beside text stating: *"Until we see it happen to the genes that do get passed down, we aren\'t going to get any deer-icorns."*\n\n**Mood:** Educational, curious, and slightly whimsical—balanced between serious scientific explanation and engaging nature photography.', 'similarity score': 0.5435241460800171}, {'clip_517_538': 'clip_517_538', 'text': '**517s–519s:** The video opens on a static, glittery pink background featuring colorful cartoon stickers of eight animals arranged around a central blue rhino. The creatures—all possessing horn-like protrusions—include a hornbill, unicorn, spiny lobster, parrotfish, rhinoceros beetle, crow, and narwhal. Between frames, the blue rhino subtly lowers its head and blinks.\n\n**521s–528s:** The scene cuts to a live-action medium shot of a bald, bearded host in a grey short-sleeved shirt with pink flamingo patterns, standing against a solid blue background. He speaks directly to the camera with friendly, engaging expressions and hand gestures. A white text banner at chest height reads **"DFTBA.com/SciShow"**, and the SciShow logo appears in the bottom-right corner.\n\n**530s–537s:** The video transitions to the end-credits sequence on a dark background. A green SciShow logo sits at the top left with "PRODUCED BY COMPLEXLY" beneath it. A bright green sidebar on the left encourages viewers to **"SUPPORT THE SHOW! patreon.com/SciShow"** and lists "Adam Crute • Science Ambassador." The right side scrolls through production credits, including hosts Reid Reimers and Madison Lynn, followed by animators, editors, producers, and Executive Producer Hank Green. The mood shifts from whimsical to informative to formal acknowledgments.', 'similarity score': 0.525547981262207}]
